  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "The fifth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is given by\n\n$$\n{P}_{5}(x)=-\\frac{1}{7}-\\frac{1}{6}(x+1)-\\frac{1}{3}(x+1)^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}(x+1)^{5}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${f}^{(5)}(-1)?$",
  "worked_solution": "The coefficient of $(x+1)^{5}$ is given by $\\frac{{f}^{(5)}(-1)}{5!}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{(5)}(-1)}{5!} & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{f}^{(5)}(-1)}{120} & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}^{(5)}(-1) & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
